(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_thedandfblockelements=[{id:"neet-2026-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Catalytic role of transition metals",q:`Match List I with List II.
List-I (Transition metal/compound complex):
A. V\u2082O\u2085
B. Fe
C. PdCl\u2082
D. Ni complex
List-II (Catalytic Role):
I. Preparation of ammonia from N\u2082/H\u2082 mixture
II. Polymerisation of alkynes
III. Preparation of H\u2082SO\u2084 and SO\u2082
IV. Oxidation of ethyne to ethanal
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:3,s:`A: V\u2082O\u2085 catalyses the oxidation of SO\u2082 to SO\u2083 in making H\u2082SO\u2084. This is III.
B: Fe is the catalyst for making ammonia from N\u2082 and H\u2082. This is I.
C: PdCl\u2082 catalyses the oxidation of ethyne to ethanal. This is IV.
D: A Ni complex catalyses polymerisation of alkynes. This is II.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2026-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Spin-only magnetic moment",q:"The calculated 'spin-only' magnetic moment of Ti\xB2\u207A (3d\xB2) is:",o:["2.84 BM","5.92 BM","4.90 BM","3.87 BM"],c:0,s:`Spin-only magnetic moment \u03BC = \u221A(n(n+2)) BM, where n is the number of unpaired electrons.
Ti\xB2\u207A has the configuration [Ar] 4s\u2070 3d\xB2, so n = 2.
\u03BC = \u221A(2\xD74) = 2.84 BM.`},{id:"neet-2026-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids: oxidation states",q:"Although +3 oxidation state is most common in lanthanoids, cerium still shows +4 oxidation state because:",o:["Its nearest inert gas is Radon.","After losing one more electron, it acquires 4f\xB9\u2074 electronic configuration.","Its atomic number is 61.","After losing one more electron, it acquires 4f\u2070 electronic configuration."],c:3,s:`+3 is the common state of lanthanoids.
Cerium also shows +4 because after losing one more electron it reaches the stable 4f\u2070 configuration.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q064",pn:64,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Thermal decomposition of KMnO\u2084",q:"The green paramagnetic species formed by heating KMnO\u2084 at 513 K is",o:["Mn\u2083O\u2084","MnO","KO\u2082","K\u2082MnO\u2084"],c:3,s:`2KMnO\u2084 \u2192 K\u2082MnO\u2084 + MnO\u2082 + O\u2082.
K\u2082MnO\u2084 forms on heating KMnO\u2084 and it is green in colour.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q068",pn:68,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Melting points of 3d metals",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: Generally, 3d transition metals have high melting points.
Reason R: Involvement of 3d-electrons in addition to 4s-electrons in the interatomic metallic bonding.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`The melting points of 3d transition metals are generally high.
This is because a greater number of electrons from (n \u2212 1)d, in addition to the ns electrons, take part in interatomic metallic bonding.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q076",pn:76,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids electronic configuration",q:`The lanthanide ion having four unpaired electrons is
(Given : Atomic numbers of Ce = 58, Nd = 60, Tb = 65 and Ho = 67)`,o:["Ce\xB3\u207A","Tb\xB3\u207A","Ho\xB3\u207A","Nd\xB3\u207A"],c:2,s:`Write the outermost configuration of each ion.
Ho\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB9\u2070: three paired orbitals and four single electrons, so 4 unpaired electrons.
Nd\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB3: 3 unpaired electrons.
Ce\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB9: 1 unpaired electron.
Tb\xB3\u207A is 4f\u2078: one paired orbital and six single electrons, so 6 unpaired electrons.
So Ho\xB3\u207A has four unpaired electrons.`,sfig:{w:260,h:172,alt:"4f orbital box diagrams: Ho\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9\u2070 has three paired and four single boxes (4 unpaired electrons); Nd\xB3\u207A 4f\xB3 has 3 and Ce\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9 has 1 unpaired electron; Tb\xB3\u207A 4f\u2078 has one paired and six single boxes (6 unpaired electrons).",d:[["t",10,30,"Ho\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9\u2070",""],["r",80,15,24,20,""],["t",92,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",104,15,24,20,""],["t",116,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",128,15,24,20,""],["t",140,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",152,15,24,20,""],["t",164,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",176,15,24,20,""],["t",188,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",200,15,24,20,""],["t",212,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",224,15,24,20,""],["t",236,30,"\u2191","m"],["t",164,52,"4 unpaired electrons","ms"],["t",10,82,"Nd\xB3\u207A 4f\xB3  3 unpaired electrons",""],["t",10,106,"Ce\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9  1 unpaired electron",""],["t",10,140,"Tb\xB3\u207A 4f\u2078",""],["r",80,125,24,20,""],["t",92,140,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",104,125,24,20,""],["t",116,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",128,125,24,20,""],["t",140,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",152,125,24,20,""],["t",164,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",176,125,24,20,""],["t",188,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",200,125,24,20,""],["t",212,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",224,125,24,20,""],["t",236,140,"\u2191","m"],["t",164,162,"6 unpaired electrons","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Magnetic properties, unpaired electrons",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Ferromagnetism is considered as an extreme form of paramagnetism.
Statement II : The number of unpaired electrons in a Cr\xB2\u207A ion (Z = 24) is the same as that of a Nd\xB3\u207A ion (Z = 60).
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],c:2,s:`Substances attracted very strongly in an applied magnetic field are called ferromagnetic.
In fact, ferromagnetism is an extreme form of paramagnetism.
Hence Statement I is correct.
Cr\xB2\u207A = 3d\u20744s\u2070
Unpaired electrons = 4
Nd\xB3\u207A = 4f\xB3 6s\u2070
Unpaired electrons = 3
Hence Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2024-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Standard electrode potentials of 3d series",q:"The E\xBA value for the Mn\xB3\u207A/Mn\xB2\u207A couple is more positive than that of Cr\xB3\u207A/Cr\xB2\u207A or Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe\xB2\u207A due to change of",o:["d\u2074 to d\u2075 configuration","d\xB3 to d\u2075 configuration","d\u2075 to d\u2074 configuration","d\u2075 to d\xB2 configuration"],c:0,s:`E\xBA(Mn\xB3\u207A/Mn\xB2\u207A) is higher than E\xBA(Cr\xB3\u207A/Cr\xB2\u207A) or E\xBA(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe\xB2\u207A).
Mn\xB3\u207A is [Ar]3d\u2074 and Mn\xB2\u207A is [Ar]3d\u2075.
Cr\xB3\u207A is [Ar]3d\xB3 and Cr\xB2\u207A is [Ar]3d\u2074.
Mn\xB3\u207A (d\u2074) gains an electron to reach the stable half-filled d\u2075 state, which has high exchange energy.
So reduction of Mn\xB3\u207A is very favourable.`},{id:"neet-2024-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Magnetic properties (spin-only moment)",q:`\u2018Spin only\u2019 magnetic moment is same for which of the following ions ?
A. Ti\xB3\u207A
B. Cr\xB2\u207A
C. Mn\xB2\u207A
D. Fe\xB2\u207A
E. Sc\xB3\u207A
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and C only","A and D only","B and D only","A and E only"],c:2,s:`Count unpaired electrons: Ti\xB3\u207A (3d\xB9) has 1; Cr\xB2\u207A (3d\u2074) has 4; Mn\xB2\u207A (3d\u2075) has 5; Fe\xB2\u207A (3d\u2076) has 4; Sc\xB3\u207A (3d\u2070) has 0.
Spin-only moment = \u221A(n(n+2)) BM.
Cr\xB2\u207A and Fe\xB2\u207A both have n = 4, so their moments are equal.`},{id:"neet-2024-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Mohr's salt preparation",q:"During the preparation of Mohr\u2019s salt solution (Ferrous ammonium sulphate), which of the following acid is added to prevent hydrolysis of Fe\xB2\u207A ion ?",o:["dilute nitric acid","dilute sulphuric acid","dilute hydrochloric acid","concentrated sulphuric acid"],c:1,s:`Dilute sulphuric acid is added while preparing Mohr\u2019s salt.
It stops the hydrolysis of Fe\xB2\u207A ion.`},{id:"neet-2024-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Magnetic properties of lanthanoids",q:"The pair of lanthanoid ions which are diamagnetic is :",o:["Gd\xB3\u207A and Eu\xB3\u207A","Pm\xB3\u207A and Sm\xB3\u207A","Ce\u2074\u207A and Yb\xB2\u207A","Ce\xB3\u207A and Eu\xB2\u207A"],c:2,s:`Magnetic moment \u03BC = \u221A(n(n+2)), n = number of unpaired electrons.
Ce\u2074\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2070, \u03BC = 0, diamagnetic.
Yb\xB2\u207A = [Xe] 4f\xB9\u2074, \u03BC = 0, diamagnetic.
Ce\xB3\u207A = [Xe] 4f\xB9, \u03BC = \u221A3, paramagnetic.
Eu\xB2\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2077, \u03BC = \u221A63, paramagnetic.
Gd\xB3\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2077, \u03BC = \u221A63, paramagnetic.
Eu\xB3\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2076, \u03BC = \u221A48, paramagnetic.
Pm\xB3\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2074, \u03BC = \u221A24, paramagnetic.
Sm\xB3\u207A = [Xe] 4f\u2075, \u03BC = \u221A35, paramagnetic.
Hence only Ce\u2074\u207A and Yb\xB2\u207A are diamagnetic.`},{id:"neet-2023-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Stability of Cu\xB2\u207A",q:"The stability of Cu\xB2\u207A is more than Cu\u207A salts in aqueous solution due to -",o:["second ionisation enthalpy.","first ionisation enthalpy.","enthalpy of atomization.","hydration energy."],c:3,s:`Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) has a much more negative hydration enthalpy than Cu\u207A(aq).
This more than makes up for the high second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.
\u0394hydH\xB0 of Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) = \u20132121 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.
\u0394iH\xB9 of Cu = +745 kJ mol\u207B\xB9 and \u0394iH\xB2 of Cu = +1960 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.`},{id:"neet-2023-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Oxides of transition metals",q:`Which of the following statements are INCORRECT ?
A. All the transition metals except scandium form MO oxides which are ionic.
B. The highest oxidation number corresponding to the group number in transition metal oxides is attained in Sc\u2082O\u2083 to Mn\u2082O\u2087.
C. Basic character increases from V\u2082O\u2083 to V\u2082O\u2084 to V\u2082O\u2085.
D. V\u2082O\u2084 dissolves in acids to give CO\u2084\xB3\u207B salts.
E. CrO is basic but Cr\u2082O\u2083 is amphoteric.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and C only","A and E only","B and D only","C and D only"],c:3,s:`A is correct: all transition metals except Sc form ionic MO oxides.
B is correct: the highest oxidation state equals the group number from Sc\u2082O\u2083 to Mn\u2082O\u2087.
C is incorrect: acidic character (not basic) increases from V\u2082O\u2083 to V\u2082O\u2084 to V\u2082O\u2085.
D is incorrect: V\u2082O\u2084 dissolves in acids to give VO\xB2\u207A salts.
E is correct: CrO is basic and Cr\u2082O\u2083 is amphoteric.
So C and D are incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2023-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Extraction of iron",q:"The reaction that does NOT take place in a blast furnace between 900 K to 1500 K temperature range during extraction of iron is :",o:["CaO + SiO\u2082 \u2192 CaSiO\u2083","Fe\u2082O\u2083 + CO \u2192 2FeO + CO\u2082","FeO + CO \u2192 Fe + CO\u2082","C + CO\u2082 \u2192 2CO"],c:1,s:`In the 900-1500 K zone, C + CO\u2082 \u2192 2CO, FeO + CO \u2192 Fe + CO\u2082 and CaO + SiO\u2082 \u2192 CaSiO\u2083 (slag) take place.
Fe\u2082O\u2083 + CO \u2192 2FeO + CO\u2082 happens at the lower range of 500-800 K.
So option 2 does not take place here.`},{id:"neet-2022-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Ionisation enthalpy of lanthanoids",q:"Gadolinium has a low value of third ionisation enthalpy because of",o:["high exchange enthalpy","high electronegativity","high basic character","small size"],c:0,s:`Gd is [Xe] 4f\u2077 5d\xB9 6s\xB2.
The third electron is removed from 5d.
This leaves the stable half-filled [Xe] 4f\u2077 configuration, which has high exchange energy.
So little energy is needed for the third ionisation.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q072",pn:72,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Actinoid contraction",q:"Decrease in size from left to right in actinoid series is greater and gradual than that in lanthanoid series due to :",o:["5 f orbitals have greater shielding effect","4 f orbitals are penultimate","4 f orbitals have greater shielding effect","5 f orbitals have poor shielding effect"],c:3,s:`5f orbitals are large and diffuse, so they shield the nuclear charge poorly.
This makes the size drop across the actinoids greater.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q089",pn:89,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Properties of transition metal ions",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Cr\xB2\u207A is oxidising and Mn\xB3\u207A is reducing in nature.
Statement II: Sc\xB3\u207A compounds are repelled by the applied magnetic field.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],c:0,s:`Cr\xB2\u207A is [Ar]3d\u2074. It becomes Cr\xB3\u207A (d\xB3, t\u2082g\xB3, stable), so Cr\xB2\u207A is reducing, not oxidising.
Mn\xB3\u207A changes to Mn\xB2\u207A (3d\u2075, extra stable), so Mn\xB3\u207A is oxidising, not reducing.
So Statement I is incorrect.
Sc\xB3\u207A is [Ar] with no unpaired electron, so it is diamagnetic and is repelled by a magnetic field.
So Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Potassium permanganate oxidising action",q:"In the neutral or faintly alkaline medium, KMnO\u2084 oxidises iodide into iodate. The change in oxidation state of manganese in this reaction is from",o:["+6 to +4","+7 to +3","+6 to +5","+7 to +4"],c:3,s:`In neutral or faintly alkaline medium: 2MnO\u2084\u207B + H\u2082O + I\u207B \u2192 2MnO\u2082 + 2OH\u207B + IO\u2083\u207B.
Mn in MnO\u2084\u207B is +7 and in MnO\u2082 is +4.
So Mn changes from +7 to +4.`},{id:"neet-2022-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Ores and their composition",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I (Ores): (a) Haematite (b) Magnetite (c) Calamine (d) Kaolinite
List-II (Composition): (i) Fe\u2083O\u2084 (ii) ZnCO\u2083 (iii) Fe\u2082O\u2083 (iv) [Al\u2082(OH)\u2084Si\u2082O\u2085]
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`Haematite is Fe\u2082O\u2083.
Magnetite is Fe\u2083O\u2084.
Calamine is ZnCO\u2083.
Kaolinite is [Al\u2082(OH)\u2084Si\u2082O\u2085].
Note: No chapter on metallurgy exists in the list; filed under the nearest chapter.`},{id:"neet-2021-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoid contraction",q:"Zr (Z = 40) and Hf (Z = 72) have similar atomic and ionic radii because of :",o:["Having similar chemical properties","Belonging to same group","Diagonal relationship","Lanthanoid contraction"],c:3,s:`Between Zr and Hf the 14 lanthanoids are filled, with poorly shielding 4f electrons.
The steady shrinking of size (lanthanoid contraction) makes the 5d element Hf almost as small as the 4d element Zr (160 pm and 159 pm).
Similar properties and same group are results, not the cause.`},{id:"neet-2021-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids and actinoids",q:"The incorrect statement among the following is :",o:["Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided","Actinoid contraction is greater for element to element than lanthanoid contraction","Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state","Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity"],c:2,s:`Option 1 is true: actinoids are reactive when finely divided.
Option 2 is true: 5f electrons shield poorly, so contraction is larger.
Option 3 is false: many trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured, in solid and in solution (f-f transitions).
Option 4 is true: they are metals.
So option 3 is the incorrect statement.`},{id:"neet-2020-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Metallurgy of iron",q:"Identify the correct statement from the following :",o:["Blister copper has blistered appearance due to evolution of CO\u2082","Vapour phase refining is carried out for Nickel by Van Arkel method","Pig iron can be moulded into a variety of shapes","Wrought iron is impure iron with 4% carbon"],c:2,s:`Blister copper looks blistered because SO\u2082 escapes, not CO\u2082.
Nickel is refined by the Mond process; the Van Arkel method is for Zr and Ti.
Pig iron (about 4% carbon) melts and can be cast into moulds.
Wrought iron is the purest form (under 0.5% carbon).
So only (3) is correct.
Note: No metallurgy chapter in the chapter list; nearest chapter used.`},{id:"neet-2020-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Magnetic moment",q:"The calculated spin only magnetic moment of Cr\xB2\u207A ion is",o:["4.90 BM","5.92 BM","2.84 BM","3.87 BM"],c:0,s:`Cr has 3d\u20754s\xB9; Cr\xB2\u207A is 3d\u2074.
Four unpaired electrons, n = 4.
\u03BC = \u221A(n(n+2)) = \u221A24 = 4.90 BM.`},{id:"neet-2020-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Transition metals",q:"Identify the incorrect statement",o:["The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity due to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes","Interstitial compounds are those that are formed when small atom like H, C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals","The oxidation states of chromium in CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B and Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B are not the same","Cr\xB2\u207A(d\u2074) is a stronger reducing agent than Fe\xB2\u207A(d\u2076) in water"],c:2,s:`In CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B: x + 4(\u22122) = \u22122, so x = +6.
In Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B: 2x + 7(\u22122) = \u22122, so x = +6.
The oxidation states are the same, so statement (3) is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2019-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Oxoanions of manganese",q:"The manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral due to -",o:["The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with p-orbtials of manganese","The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of d-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese","The \u03C0-bonding involves overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen with d-orbitals of manganese","There is no \u03C0-bonding"],c:2,s:`Oxygen has no d orbitals in its valence shell, so the second option is not possible.
In MnO\u2084\u207B and MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B the \u03C0 bonds form by overlap of filled p orbitals of oxygen with empty d orbitals of manganese (p\u03C0\u2013d\u03C0).
This gives a tetrahedral shape.`,sfig:{w:180,h:205,alt:"Tetrahedral permanganate MnO\u2084\u207B and manganate MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B ions drawn with Mn at the centre, with the p\u03C0\u2013d\u03C0 overlap labelled on the Mn=O bonds and O\u2296 on the single-bonded oxygen.",d:[["t",46,54,"Mn","m"],["t",46,22,"O","m"],["t",53,18,"p\u03C0",""],["l",47.8,28,47.8,40,""],["l",44.2,28,44.2,40,""],["t",53,40,"d\u03C0",""],["t",20,75,"O","m"],["l",37.1,57.5,26.1,65.5,""],["l",34.9,54.5,23.9,62.5,""],["t",46,83,"O","m"],["l",47.8,59,47.8,71,""],["l",44.2,59,44.2,71,""],["t",78,68,"O","m"],["t",83,60,"\u207B",""],["l",60,52,73,60,""],["t",106,55,"MnO\u2084\u207B",""],["t",58,162,"Mn","m"],["t",56,130,"O","m"],["t",63,125,"p\u03C0",""],["l",59.8,136,59.8,148,""],["l",56.2,136,56.2,148,""],["t",64,148,"d\u03C0",""],["t",42,162,"d\u03C0","e"],["t",20,173,"p\u03C0","e"],["t",28,182,"O","m"],["l",47,166.5,35,174.5,""],["l",45,163.5,33,171.5,""],["t",57,192,"O","m"],["t",63,192,"\u207B",""],["l",59.8,168,59.8,180,""],["l",56.2,168,56.2,180,""],["t",80,177,"O","m"],["t",84,169,"\u207B",""],["l",64,163,76,170,""],["t",118,176,"MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B",""]]}},{id:"neet-2019-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Ores of metals",q:"Which one is malachite from the following?",o:["Fe\u2083O\u2084","CuCO\u2083\xB7Cu(OH)\u2082","CuFeS\u2082","Cu(OH)\u2082"],c:1,s:`Malachite is the green basic carbonate of copper, CuCO\u2083\xB7Cu(OH)\u2082.
Fe\u2083O\u2084 is magnetite and CuFeS\u2082 is copper pyrites.`},{id:"neet-2018-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"d-d transitions and magnetism",q:"Which one of the following ions exhibits d-d transition and paramagnetism as well ?",o:["CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B","Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B","MnO\u2084\u207B","MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B"],c:3,s:`d-d transitions and paramagnetism both need partly filled d orbitals, not d\u2070 or d\xB9\u2070.
CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B and Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B: Cr is +6, d\u2070. MnO\u2084\u207B: Mn is +7, d\u2070.
MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B: Mn is +6, [Ar]3d\xB9, one unpaired electron.
So only MnO\u2084\xB2\u207B is paramagnetic and shows d-d transition.`},{id:"neet-2017-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Actinoids: oxidation states",q:"The reason for greater range of oxidation states in actinoids is attributed to :",o:["4f and 5d levels being close in energies","the radioactive nature of actinoids","actinoid contraction","5f, 6d and 7s levels having comparable energies"],c:3,s:`In actinoids the 5f, 6d and 7s energy levels are very close.
So electrons from all three can take part in bonding.
This gives many oxidation states.`},{id:"neet-2017-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Extraction of silver",q:"Extraction of gold and silver involves leaching with CN\u207B ion. Silver is later recovered by :",o:["displacement with Zn","liquation","distillation","zone refining"],c:0,s:`Silver ore is leached with NaCN solution, forming the soluble complex Na[Ag(CN)2].
Zinc is more reactive than silver, so it displaces silver from the complex.
Reaction: 2Na[Ag(CN)2] + Zn gives Na2[Zn(CN)4] + 2Ag.`,sfig:{w:290,h:110,alt:"Ag\u2082S with NaCN gives Na[Ag(CN)\u2082] + Na\u2082S; then Zn gives Ag + Na\u2082[Zn(CN)\u2084]",d:[["t",10,30,"Ag\u2082S",""],["l",50,26,100,26,"a"],["t",75,18,"NaCN","ms"],["t",106,30,"Na [Ag(CN)\u2082] + Na\u2082S",""],["l",130,40,130,70,"a"],["t",138,60,"Zn",""],["t",106,95,"Ag + Na\u2082 [Zn(CN)\u2084]",""]]}},{id:"neet-2017-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Mercury and iodine complexes",q:"HgCl2 and I2 both when dissolved in water containing I\u207B ions pair of species formed is :",o:["Hg2I2, I\u207B","HgI2, I3\u207B","HgI2, I\u207B","HgI4\xB2\u207B, I3\u207B"],c:3,s:`HgCl2 reacts with I\u207B to give HgI2 (red precipitate).
With excess I\u207B it dissolves as the complex ion: HgI2 + 2I\u207B gives [HgI4]\xB2\u207B.
I2 dissolves in I\u207B solution by forming I3\u207B.
So the pair formed is HgI4\xB2\u207B and I3\u207B.`},{id:"neet-2016-q008",pn:8,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Electronic configuration of lanthanoids",q:"The electronic configurations of Eu (Atomic No. 63), Gd(Atomic No. 64) and Tb (Atomic No. 65) are:",o:["[Xe]4f\u20765d\xB96s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20765d\xB96s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20785d\xB96s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20786s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20785d\xB96s\xB2"],c:2,s:`Eu (63): [Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, a half-filled 4f subshell, which is extra stable.
Gd (64): [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2, the extra electron goes to 5d to keep 4f half-filled.
Tb (65): [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2.
This matches option (3).`},{id:"neet-2016-q015",pn:15,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Metallurgy processes",q:`Match items of Column I with the items of Column II and assign the correct code:
Column I: (a) Cyanide process  (b) Froth floatation process  (c) Electrolytic reduction  (d) Zone refining
Column II: (i) Ultrapure Ge  (ii) Dressing of ZnS  (iii) Extraction of Al  (iv) Extraction of Au  (v) Purification of Ni`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(v)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(v), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Cyanide process: gold is dissolved as Na[Au(CN)\u2082] and then displaced by Zn, so it is used for extraction of Au.
4Au + 8NaCN + O\u2082 + 2H\u2082O \u2192 4Na[Au(CN)\u2082] + 4NaOH
2Na[Au(CN)\u2082] + Zn \u2192 2Au + Na\u2082[Zn(CN)\u2084]
Froth floatation: sulphide ores such as ZnS are concentrated (dressing).
Electrolytic reduction (Hall-H\xE9roult, with cryolite): extraction of Al.
Zone refining: semiconductors like Ge and Si are made ultrapure.
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i).
Note: Chapter placed in the d- and f-Block list because no metallurgy chapter exists in the NEET chapter list.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q074",pn:74,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids",q:"Which one of the following statements related to lanthanons is incorrect ?",o:["Europium shows +2 oxidation state","The basicity decreases as the ionic radius decreases from Pr to Lu","All the lanthanons are much more reactive than aluminium","Ce (+4) solutions are widely used as oxidizing agent in volumetric analysis"],c:2,s:`(1) Eu\xB2\u207A has the stable 4f\u2077 configuration, so +2 is shown.
(2) Basicity falls as ionic radius falls from Pr to Lu (lanthanoid contraction).
(4) Ce\u2074\u207A is a strong oxidant used in titrations.
(3) is wrong: the early lanthanoids are very reactive (like calcium), but with higher atomic number they become more like aluminium, so not all are much more reactive than Al.`}]})();
